= i
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Here $ZF^*$ denotes <ZF without foundation>. <Transfinite recursion> on <ordinals> defines the <cumulative hierarchy> by
$$
\boxed{V_0=\varnothing,\qquad V_{\alpha+1}=\mathcal P(V_\alpha),\qquad V_\lambda=\bigcup_{\beta<\lambda}V_\beta\quad(\lambda\text{ a nonzero limit}).}
$$
The associated class is $V=\{x:\exists\alpha\in\operatorname{Ord}\ (x\in V_\alpha)\}$. Each level is a set, while $V$ is a definable class. Without <Foundation> it need not contain every ambient set; it consists of the sets built by this <well-founded> hierarchy.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use <transfinite induction>. The inclusion at zero is immediate. If $V_\beta\subseteq V_{\beta+1}$, monotonicity of the <power set> gives
$$
V_{\beta+1}=\mathcal P(V_\beta)\subseteq\mathcal P(V_{\beta+1})=V_{\beta+2}.
$$
If $\alpha$ is a limit and $x\in V_\alpha$, choose $\beta<\alpha$ with $x\in V_\beta$. The inductive inclusion $V_\beta\subseteq\mathcal P(V_\beta)$ means that $x\subseteq V_\beta\subseteq V_\alpha$. Thus $x\in\mathcal P(V_\alpha)=V_{\alpha+1}$. This proves **$V_\alpha\subseteq V_{\alpha+1}$ for every <ordinal>**. These successive inclusions and the union definition also give $V_\beta\subseteq V_\alpha$ whenever $\beta\leq\alpha$.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The preceding inclusion is exactly $V_\alpha\subseteq\mathcal P(V_\alpha)$. Therefore every $x\in V_\alpha$ is a subset of $V_\alpha$. Equivalently,
$$
\boxed{y\in x\in V_\alpha\Longrightarrow y\in V_\alpha,}
$$
which says that $V_\alpha$ is a <transitive set>. This uses the <power-set> definition and the proved inclusion, without any ambient use of <Foundation>.

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

We prove the stronger <ordinal> identity
$$
V_\alpha\cap\operatorname{Ord}=\alpha
$$
by <transfinite induction>. It holds at zero. At a successor stage an <ordinal> $\gamma$ belongs to $\mathcal P(V_\beta)$ exactly when every $\delta<\gamma$ belongs to $V_\beta$. By induction this means every $\delta<\gamma$ is less than $\beta$, equivalently $\gamma\leq\beta$. Thus the <ordinals> in $V_{\beta+1}$ are precisely those less than $\beta+1$. At a limit the identity follows by taking unions.

Every $\beta<\alpha$ consequently belongs to $V_\alpha$, so $\alpha\subseteq V_\alpha$ and $\alpha\in\mathcal P(V_\alpha)=V_{\alpha+1}$. But $\alpha\notin V_\alpha$, since an <ordinal> is not less than itself. Hence
$$
\boxed{\alpha\in V_{\alpha+1}\setminus V_\alpha.}
$$
The only well-foundedness used is that of the <ordinal> order, available independently of <Foundation> for arbitrary ambient sets.

= d
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Every set is a subset of itself, so $V_\alpha\subseteq V_\alpha$. By the successor definition,
$$
\boxed{V_\alpha\in\mathcal P(V_\alpha)=V_{\alpha+1}.}
$$
Membership and inclusion are different statements here; this argument establishes membership of the whole level in its successor.

= iii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The class $V$ is transitive because all its levels are transitive. Let $a\in V$ be nonempty. There is a least <ordinal> $\alpha$ for which $a\cap V_\alpha\ne\varnothing$: existence follows by choosing an element of $a$, which lies in $V$, and then minimizing within some witnessing <ordinal> bound. The least <ordinal> is neither zero nor a limit, so write $\alpha=\beta+1$. Choose $x\in a\cap V_{\beta+1}$. Then $x\subseteq V_\beta$, while minimality gives $a\cap V_\beta=\varnothing$. Thus $a\cap x=\varnothing$. Also $x\in V$, proving **<Foundation> inside $\langle V,\in\rangle$**.

By the given hypothesis this same class satisfies every axiom of $ZF^*$, and hence it satisfies every axiom of $ZF$. Translate any finite formal $ZF$ proof by restricting each <quantifier> to the definable class $V$. Within $ZF^*$ the translated axioms have proofs, logical steps remain valid, and the translated contradiction is still a contradiction. Substituting those axiom proofs therefore converts the original derivation effectively into a finite derivation of contradiction from $ZF^*$. Equivalently,
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Con}(ZF^*)\Longrightarrow\operatorname{Con}(ZF).}
$$
This is the syntactic relative-consistency construction; it does not assume that $V$ is a set or that it equals the whole ambient universe.

= 6
{parent=2007/B}
